\title{The Exact Off-Resonance Window Formula:\\
Sinc Laws, Sidelobe Sharpness, and their Arithmetic Shadow}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, exact analysis of the rectangularly windowed tone
\[
W(T,\omega) \;=\; \int_{-T}^{T} e^{i\omega t}\,dt,
\]
the fundamental object underlying finite-time frequency measurement. We prove
the closed form $W(T,\omega) = 2\sin(\omega T)/\omega$ for $\omega\neq0$ and
$W(T,0)=2T$ at resonance, unified as the \emph{sinc law}
$W(T,\omega)=2T\sinc(\omega T)$, and derive from it the complete quantitative
geometry of the response: global peak dominance $|W|\le 2T$; the sidelobe
envelope $|W|\le 2/|\omega|$ \emph{together with its sharpness}, equality
holding at every half-integer detuning $\omega=(2k+1)\pi/(2T)$; the exact zero
set $\omega=k\pi/T$ ($k\neq0$) with strict positivity throughout the main lobe
$0<\omega T<\pi$; a quadratic main-lobe lower bound
$|W|\ge 2T\bigl(1-(\omega T)^2/4\bigr)$ for $|\omega T|\le 1$; and a resolution
bound stating that half of the peak amplitude is retained only for
$|\omega|\le 2/T$.

We then develop the arithmetic counterpart --- the exponential sum
$S_N(\alpha)=\sum_{n<N}e^{2\pi i n\alpha}$ --- proving the Dirichlet-kernel
modulus law $|S_N(\alpha)|=|\sin(\pi N\alpha)|/|\sin(\pi\alpha)|$, the classical
bound $|S_N(\alpha)|\le 1/(2\nrm{\alpha})$, a Jordan main-lobe bound
$|S_N(\alpha)|\ge (2/\pi)N$ for $|\alpha|\le 1/(2N)$ with the constant $2/\pi$
shown to be exactly attained in the limit, Weyl cancellation for irrational
frequencies, and an arithmetic resonance theorem derived from Dirichlet
approximation. An exact \emph{sampling bridge} $C_N(\alpha)=S_N(\alpha)C_1(\alpha)$
factors the continuous window through the exponential sum, and the modulus is
invariant under recentring.

Two applications close the paper. A sharp Rayleigh criterion identifies an
exact critical time--bandwidth product $c\in[4.2,4.4)$ (numerically
$c\approx 4.27836$) below which the midpoint of two equal tones is strictly
brighter than a tone centre and above which it is strictly darker; the proof
rests on a complete sign analysis of $G(x)=\sin x\,(2-\cos x)-x$, whose
derivative $G'(x)=2\cos x\,(1-\cos x)$ makes the monotonicity transparent. A
Fej\'er identity rewrites $|S_N(\alpha)|^2$ as a triangularly weighted cosine
polynomial, yielding nonnegativity, total mass $N$, and uniform-in-$N$
concentration $|S_N(\alpha)|^2\le 1/(4\delta^2)$ for $\nrm{\alpha}\ge\delta$.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} sinc kernel, Dirichlet kernel, Fej\'er kernel,
exponential sums, Weyl equidistribution, spectral leakage, Rayleigh criterion,
time--bandwidth product.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement problem}

Finite observation is the universal constraint of frequency measurement. A
pure tone of angular frequency $\omega_0$, observed only on a bounded time
interval, cannot be distinguished from a tone of frequency
$\omega_0+\varepsilon$ for sufficiently small $\varepsilon$. Quantifying
``sufficiently small'' is the content of this paper.

The canonical model is a matched-filter correlation: multiply by the conjugate
candidate and integrate over the observation window. For a pure tone the result
depends only on the \emph{detuning} $\omega$ and on the window. For the
symmetric rectangular window of half-width $T$ this is the \emph{windowed tone}
\begin{equation}
W(T,\omega) \;=\; \int_{-T}^{T} e^{i\omega t}\,dt.
\end{equation}
It is elementary that $W(T,0)=2T$ and $|W(T,\omega)|<2T$ for $\omega\neq0$,
and this qualitative statement is occasionally offered as ``the resonance
theorem''. Our thesis is that the qualitative statement is nearly useless in
practice, and that the correct object of study is the exact identity, from
which every practically relevant quantity --- peak width, first-zero location,
sidelobe amplitude, resolution threshold --- follows by computation.

\subsection{The arithmetic shadow}

The same kernel governs a superficially unrelated subject. In analytic number
theory one studies the exponential sum
$S_N(\alpha)=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}e^{2\pi i n\alpha}$, which controls
equidistribution, Weyl's criterion, the circle method, and the theory of Fourier
series. $S_N$ is the sampled version of the same window, and the two are related
not by analogy but by an exact factorization (Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge}).

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $T>0$ is a window half-width, $\omega\in\R$ an angular detuning,
$\alpha\in\R$ a frequency in cycles per unit time, and $N\in\N$ a sample count.

\begin{definition}[Cardinal sine]
$\sinc(x)=\sin x/x$ for $x\neq0$, and $\sinc(0)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Windowed tone]
$W(T,\omega)=\displaystyle\int_{-T}^{T}e^{i\omega t}\,dt$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Exponential sum]
$S_N(\alpha)=\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}e^{2\pi i n\alpha}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Continuous window in cycle units]
$C_b(\alpha)=\displaystyle\int_0^b e^{2\pi i\alpha s}\,ds$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Distance to the nearest integer]
$\nrm{\alpha}=|\alpha-\operatorname{round}(\alpha)|$, so $0\le\nrm{\alpha}\le1/2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Two-tone response]
$R_{T,\Delta}(\omega)=W\bigl(T,\omega-\tfrac{\Delta}{2}\bigr)+W\bigl(T,\omega+\tfrac{\Delta}{2}\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rayleigh gap function]
$G(x)=\sin x\,(2-\cos x)-x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sincbasic}
$\sinc$ is even and $|\sinc(x)|\le1$ for all $x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Evenness follows from $\sin(-x)=-\sin x$, the value at $0$ matching by
definition. For $x\neq0$, $|\sinc(x)|=|\sin x|/|x|\le 1$ since $|\sin x|\le|x|$;
the case $x=0$ is an equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Quadratic lower bound]\label{lem:sincquad}
For $|x|\le1$, $\;\sinc(x)\ge 1-\dfrac{x^2}{4}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By evenness assume $0<x\le1$. The refined Maclaurin estimate
$\sin x>x-x^3/4$ on $(0,1]$ gives $\sin x/x>1-x^2/4$; the case $x=0$ is an
equality.
\end{proof}

\section{The continuous window: exact law and quantitative geometry}

\subsection{The sinc law}

\begin{theorem}[Resonance value]\label{thm:res}
For every $T$, $\;W(T,0)=2T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The integrand is identically $1$ and $\int_{-T}^{T}1\,dt=2T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact off-resonance formula]\label{thm:off}
For $\omega\neq0$ and any $T$,
\[
W(T,\omega)=\frac{2\sin(\omega T)}{\omega}.
\]
In particular $W(T,\omega)$ is real.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $i\omega\neq0$, the antiderivative of $e^{i\omega t}$ is
$e^{i\omega t}/(i\omega)$, so $W=\bigl(e^{i\omega T}-e^{-i\omega T}\bigr)/(i\omega)$.
Writing $e^{\pm i\omega T}=\cos(\omega T)\pm i\sin(\omega T)$, the cosine terms
cancel and the sine terms double, giving $2i\sin(\omega T)/(i\omega)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The sinc law]\label{thm:sinclaw}
For all $T,\omega\in\R$, $\;W(T,\omega)=2T\sinc(\omega T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\omega=0$ this is Theorem~\ref{thm:res}. If $T=0$ both sides vanish.
Otherwise $\omega T\neq0$ and
$2T\sinc(\omega T)=2T\sin(\omega T)/(\omega T)=2\sin(\omega T)/\omega$, which is
Theorem~\ref{thm:off}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:sinclaw} is the organizing identity of the paper: the apparent
case split is an artefact of the $0/0$ form.

\begin{corollary}[Reality]
$\operatorname{Im}W(T,\omega)=0$: the symmetric rectangular window produces a
purely real response.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Peak dominance}

\begin{theorem}[Peak dominance]\label{thm:peak}
For $T\ge0$ and all $\omega$, $\;|W(T,\omega)|\le 2T$, with equality at
$\omega=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorems~\ref{thm:sinclaw} and Lemma~\ref{lem:sincbasic},
$|W|=2T|\sinc(\omega T)|\le 2T$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Sidelobes and their sharpness}

\begin{theorem}[Sidelobe envelope]\label{thm:side}
For $\omega\neq0$ and any $T$, $\;|W(T,\omega)|\le \dfrac{2}{|\omega|}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|W|=2|\sin(\omega T)|/|\omega|\le 2/|\omega|$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:off}.
\end{proof}

The window length is absent from the bound: this is the mathematical content of
\emph{spectral leakage}. Increasing $T$ raises the peak but does not lower the
far sidelobes; only tapering the window can.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the sidelobe envelope]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $T>0$, $k\in\Z$, and $\omega_k=\dfrac{(2k+1)\pi}{2T}$. Then
\[
|W(T,\omega_k)|=\frac{2}{|\omega_k|}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
One computes $\omega_k T=k\pi+\pi/2$, whence
$\sin(\omega_k T)=\sin(k\pi)\cos(\pi/2)+\cos(k\pi)\sin(\pi/2)=(-1)^k$, so
$|\sin(\omega_k T)|=1$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:off} gives $|W|=2/|\omega_k|$.
\end{proof}

The $\omega_k$ are exactly the midpoints between consecutive zeros
(Theorem~\ref{thm:zeros}), i.e.\ the sidelobe crests. So $2/|\omega|$ is touched
once inside every sidelobe, and no envelope $c/|\omega|$ with $c<2$ is valid.

\subsection{The zero set and the main lobe}

\begin{theorem}[Exact zero set]\label{thm:zeros}
Let $T>0$ and $\omega\neq0$. Then $W(T,\omega)=0$ if and only if
$\omega=k\pi/T$ for some nonzero integer $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:off}, $W=0$ iff $\sin(\omega T)=0$, i.e.\ $\omega T=k\pi$;
$k=0$ is excluded since $\omega\neq0$, $T\neq0$. Conversely $\omega=k\pi/T$
gives $\sin(k\pi)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No zero inside the main lobe]\label{thm:mainpos}
If $T>0$, $\omega>0$ and $\omega T<\pi$, then $W(T,\omega)>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sin$ is strictly positive on $(0,\pi)$, so $\sin(\omega T)>0$; divide by
$\omega>0$.
\end{proof}

Thus $\pi/T$ is genuinely the \emph{first} zero, and full-width at first null is
exactly $2\pi/T$ --- a claim no bound alone can make.

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative main lobe]\label{thm:mainlow}
For $T\ge0$ and $|\omega T|\le1$,
\[
|W(T,\omega)|\ \ge\ 2T\left(1-\frac{(\omega T)^2}{4}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:sinclaw} with Lemma~\ref{lem:sincquad} at $x=\omega T$;
since $|x|\le1$ forces $\sinc(x)\ge3/4>0$, the modulus equals
$2T\sinc(\omega T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution bound]\label{thm:resol}
Let $T>0$. If $|W(T,\omega)|\ge T$ --- half of the peak amplitude $2T$ --- then
$|\omega|\le 2/T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $\omega=0$ this is trivial. Otherwise $T\le|W|\le2/|\omega|$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:side}, so $|\omega|T\le2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:resol} is the time--frequency uncertainty principle in
elementary form: the half-amplitude bandwidth of a rectangular observation of
total duration $2T$ is $O(1/T)$. With Theorem~\ref{thm:mainlow} it brackets the
peak width from both sides.
\end{remark}

\section{The arithmetic shadow: exponential sums}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete resonance]\label{thm:dres}
For every $N$ and $k\in\Z$, $\;S_N(k)=N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:expmod}
For real $\theta$, $\;|e^{i\theta}-1|=2|\sin(\theta/2)|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$e^{i\theta}-1=2i\,e^{i\theta/2}\sin(\theta/2)$; take moduli.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dirichlet-kernel law]\label{thm:dirichlet}
If $\alpha\notin\Z$ then
\[
|S_N(\alpha)|=\frac{|\sin(\pi N\alpha)|}{|\sin(\pi\alpha)|}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$S_N$ is a geometric series with ratio $q=e^{2\pi i\alpha}\neq1$, so
$S_N=(q^N-1)/(q-1)$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:expmod} to numerator
($\theta=2\pi N\alpha$) and denominator ($\theta=2\pi\alpha$); the factors of
$2$ cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete sidelobe bound]\label{thm:dside}
For $\alpha\notin\Z$, $\;|S_N(\alpha)|\le 1/|\sin(\pi\alpha)|$, uniformly in $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Jordan's inequality on the circle]\label{thm:jordan}
For all $\alpha\in\R$, $\;2\nrm{\alpha}\le|\sin(\pi\alpha)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides are $1$-periodic and even, so assume $0\le\alpha\le1/2$, where
$\nrm{\alpha}=\alpha$. Concavity of $\sin$ on $[0,\pi]$ gives
$\sin(\pi\alpha)\ge2\alpha$ there.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classical exponential-sum bound]\label{thm:classical}
If $\nrm{\alpha}>0$ then $\;|S_N(\alpha)|\le \dfrac{1}{2\nrm{\alpha}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:dside} and \ref{thm:jordan}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Weyl cancellation]\label{thm:weyl}
If $\alpha\notin\Z$ then $|S_N(\alpha)|/N\to0$. Consequently, if $\alpha$ is
irrational and $h\in\Z\setminus\{0\}$ then $|S_N(h\alpha)|/N\to0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:dside} the numerator is bounded by the constant
$1/|\sin(\pi\alpha)|$; divide by $N\to\infty$. For the second claim, $h\alpha$
is irrational, hence non-integral.
\end{proof}

By Weyl's criterion this is exactly the statement that $(n\alpha)_{n\ge0}$ is
equidistributed modulo $1$ for irrational $\alpha$.

\begin{theorem}[Jordan main-lobe bound]\label{thm:jml}
For $|\alpha|\le \dfrac{1}{2N}$, $\;|S_N(\alpha)|\ge \dfrac{2}{\pi}N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On this range $|\pi N\alpha|\le\pi/2$, so $\sin u\ge(2/\pi)u$ gives
$|\sin(\pi N\alpha)|\ge 2N|\alpha|$, while $|\sin(\pi\alpha)|\le\pi|\alpha|$.
Divide, using Theorem~\ref{thm:dirichlet}; the case $\alpha=0$ is
Theorem~\ref{thm:dres}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact edge value and sharpness of $2/\pi$]\label{thm:sharp2pi}
For $N\ge1$,
\[
\Bigl|S_N\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2N}\bigr)\Bigr|=\frac{1}{\sin\bigl(\pi/(2N)\bigr)},
\qquad
\frac{\bigl|S_N\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2N}\bigr)\bigr|}{N}\longrightarrow\frac{2}{\pi}.
\]
Moreover $\bigl|S_N(1/(2N))\bigr|\le \tfrac{2}{\pi}N+\tfrac{1}{N}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:dirichlet} with $\alpha=1/(2N)$ makes the numerator
$|\sin(\pi/2)|=1$. Then the ratio equals
$\bigl(2/\pi\bigr)/\sinc\bigl(\pi/(2N)\bigr)$, and $\sinc$ is continuous with
$\sinc(0)=1$.
\end{proof}

Thus Theorem~\ref{thm:jml} is asymptotically optimal.

\begin{theorem}[Discrete resolution bound]
If $N\ge1$ and $|S_N(\alpha)|\ge N/2$ then $\nrm{\alpha}\le 1/N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:classical}, $N/2\le 1/(2\nrm{\alpha})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic resonance]\label{thm:arith}
For every real $\alpha$ and every $N\ge1$ there is an integer $q$ with
$1\le q\le N$ such that
\[
|S_M(q\alpha)|\ \ge\ \frac{2}{\pi}M \qquad\text{for all } M \text{ with } 2M\le N+1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Dirichlet approximation supplies $q\le N$ and $p\in\Z$ with
$\nrm{q\alpha}\le1/(N+1)$. By $1$-periodicity of $S_M$ in the frequency, replace
$q\alpha$ by its representative $\beta$ with $|\beta|=\nrm{q\alpha}$. For
$2M\le N+1$ this gives $|\beta|\le1/(2M)$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:jml} applies at
sample count $M$.
\end{proof}

\section{The sampling bridge}

\begin{lemma}[One sampling cell]\label{lem:cell}
For all $c,\alpha\in\R$,
$\displaystyle\int_c^{c+1}e^{2\pi i\alpha s}\,ds=e^{2\pi i\alpha c}\,C_1(\alpha)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $s=c+u$; the phase factorizes and the constant pulls out.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sampling bridge: Dirichlet $\times$ sinc]\label{thm:bridge}
For every $N\in\N$ and $\alpha\in\R$,
\[
C_N(\alpha)=S_N(\alpha)\cdot C_1(\alpha),\qquad\text{hence}\qquad
|C_N(\alpha)|=|S_N(\alpha)|\,|C_1(\alpha)|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split $[0,N]$ into unit cells $[n,n+1]$, $n<N$, apply Lemma~\ref{lem:cell} to
each, and sum: $C_N=\sum_{n<N}e^{2\pi i\alpha n}C_1=S_N C_1$. Formally this is
an induction on $N$ with the cell lemma as inductive step.
\end{proof}

This is the exact form of ``a sampled measurement is a Dirichlet comb times a
sinc envelope'': the sum supplies the periodic resonance structure, the single
cell the non-periodic envelope that suppresses aliases.

\begin{theorem}[Exact continuous modulus and recentring invariance]
For $\alpha\neq0$, $\;|C_b(\alpha)|=\dfrac{|\sin(\pi\alpha b)|}{\pi|\alpha|}$,
and for all $b,\alpha$,
\[
|C_b(\alpha)|=\bigl|W\bigl(\tfrac{b}{2},\,2\pi\alpha\bigr)\bigr|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Translating the window multiplies the integral by a unimodular phase, which the
modulus discards; substituting $\omega=2\pi\alpha$ converts cycle units into
angular units. Both sides equal $|\sin(\pi\alpha b)|/(\pi|\alpha|)$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:off}.
\end{proof}

\section{A sharp Rayleigh criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Sinc form of the two-tone response]
\[
R_{T,\Delta}(\omega)=2T\Bigl[\sinc\bigl((\omega-\tfrac{\Delta}{2})T\bigr)
+\sinc\bigl((\omega+\tfrac{\Delta}{2})T\bigr)\Bigr].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Values at the tone centre and midpoint]\label{thm:vals}
For $\Delta>0$,
\[
R_{T,\Delta}\bigl(\tfrac{\Delta}{2}\bigr)=\frac{2\Delta T+2\sin(\Delta T)}{\Delta},
\qquad
R_{T,\Delta}(0)=\frac{8\sin(\Delta T/2)}{\Delta}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $\omega=\Delta/2$ the first term is $2T\sinc(0)=2T$ and the second is
$2\sin(\Delta T)/\Delta$. At $\omega=0$ the arguments are $\mp\Delta T/2$ and
evenness of $\sinc$ makes the two terms equal, giving $4T\sinc(\Delta T/2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Resolution]
Two tones separated by $\Delta$ are \emph{resolved} by the window of half-width
$T$ if $R_{T,\Delta}(0)<R_{T,\Delta}(\Delta/2)$: the midpoint is strictly darker
than a tone centre.
\end{definition}

Put $x=\Delta T/2$. Using $\sin(2x)=2\sin x\cos x$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:vals}
and multiplying by $\Delta/4>0$, the resolution condition becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rescond}
2\sin x < x+\sin x\cos x
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\sin x\,(2-\cos x)<x
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
G(x)<0 .
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Derivative]\label{lem:Gprime}
$G'(x)=2\cos x\,(1-\cos x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\frac{d}{dx}\bigl[\sin x(2-\cos x)\bigr]=\cos x(2-\cos x)+\sin^2x
=2\cos x-2\cos^2x+1$; subtract the derivative $1$ of $x$.
\end{proof}

Since $1-\cos x\ge0$, the sign of $G'$ is that of $\cos x$; this single
observation drives the whole analysis.

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity and boundary values]\label{lem:mono}
$G$ is strictly increasing on $[0,\pi/2]$ and strictly decreasing on
$[\pi/2,\pi]$, with $G(0)=0$, $G(\pi/2)=2>0$, $G(\pi)=-\pi<0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Exact critical scale]\label{thm:xc}
There is a unique $x_c$ with $2.1\le x_c<2.2$ such that
\[
G(x)>0 \ \ (0<x<x_c),\qquad G(x_c)=0,\qquad G(x)<0 \ \ (x>x_c).
\]
Numerically $x_c=2.13918\ldots$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On $(0,\pi/2]$, $G$ increases strictly from $G(0)=0$, hence is positive. On
$[\pi/2,\pi]$ it decreases strictly from $2$ to $-\pi$, so by the intermediate
value theorem it has a unique zero $x_c$ there, positive before and negative
after. Certified numeric bounds give $G(2.1)\ge0>G(2.2)$, locating
$x_c\in[2.1,2.2)$. For $x\ge\pi$ the inequality persists crudely, since
$\sin x(2-\cos x)\le 3<\pi\le x$; the delicate strip $[2.2,\pi]$ is covered by
strict antitonicity together with $G(2.2)<0$, sharpened where needed by a cubic
upper bound for $\cos$ derived from $\cos x=1-2\sin^2(x/2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp Rayleigh criterion]\label{thm:rayleigh}
Let $T>0$. There is an exact critical time--bandwidth product
\[
c=2x_c\in[4.2,\ 4.4),\qquad c\approx 4.27836,
\]
such that for every $\Delta>0$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $\Delta T<c$ then $R_{T,\Delta}(\Delta/2)<R_{T,\Delta}(0)$ --- the
midpoint is strictly brighter and the pair is unresolved;
\item if $\Delta T>c$ then $R_{T,\Delta}(0)<R_{T,\Delta}(\Delta/2)$ --- the
midpoint is strictly darker and the pair is resolved.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set $x=\Delta T/2$, so $\Delta T\lessgtr c\iff x\lessgtr x_c$. By
\eqref{eq:rescond} the two claims are $G(x)>0$ and $G(x)<0$, which is
Theorem~\ref{thm:xc}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every $T>0$: no resolution occurs when $\Delta T\le4.2$, and resolution is
strict as soon as $\Delta T\ge4.4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect resolution at the first null]\label{thm:null}
For $T>0$ and $\Delta=2\pi/T$,
\[
R_{T,\Delta}(0)=0,\qquad R_{T,\Delta}(\pi/T)=2T.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sin(\Delta T/2)=\sin\pi=0$ kills the midpoint. At $\omega=\pi/T=\Delta/2$ the
second term has argument $2\pi$ with $\sin(2\pi)=0$, so only $2T$ survives.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Since $2\pi\approx6.283>c\approx4.278$, resolvability sets in strictly earlier
than perfect separation: there is a genuine intermediate regime
$4.278<\Delta T<6.283$ in which the tones are distinguishable but their
sidelobes still interfere.
\end{remark}

\section{The Fej\'er identity: energy, mass, and concentration}

\begin{theorem}[Fej\'er's triangular identity]\label{thm:fejer}
For every $N\in\N$ and $\alpha\in\R$,
\[
|S_N(\alpha)|^2=2\sum_{d=0}^{N-1}(N-d)\cos(2\pi d\alpha)-N .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expand $|S_N|^2=\sum_{m,n<N}e^{2\pi i(m-n)\alpha}$ and group by the lag
$d=m-n$: there are $N-|d|$ pairs at lag $d$, and conjugate lags combine into
cosines, giving $N+2\sum_{d=1}^{N-1}(N-d)\cos(2\pi d\alpha)$. The stated form
merely absorbs the $d=0$ term ($=2N$) into the sum and compensates with $-N$.
Formally the identity is an induction on $N$, the step being the cross-term
computation
$\operatorname{Re}\bigl(S_N(\alpha)\overline{e^{2\pi iN\alpha}}\bigr)
=\sum_{n<N}\cos\bigl(2\pi(N-n)\alpha\bigr)$ combined with an index reflection.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fej\'er positivity]
$2\sum_{d<N}(N-d)\cos(2\pi d\alpha)-N\ge0$ for all $N,\alpha$.
\end{corollary}

This is non-obvious as a statement about triangularly weighted cosine
polynomials; the analogous statement with constant weights (the Dirichlet
kernel) is false, which is exactly why Fej\'er means converge where Fourier
partial sums may not.

Write $K_N(\alpha)=|S_N(\alpha)|^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Total mass]
$\displaystyle\int_0^1 K_N(\alpha)\,d\alpha=N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Integrate Theorem~\ref{thm:fejer} termwise: every harmonic with $d\ge1$ has zero
mean over a period, leaving $2N-N=N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform concentration]
If $\delta>0$ and $\nrm{\alpha}\ge\delta$ then $K_N(\alpha)\le \dfrac{1}{4\delta^2}$,
uniformly in $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|S_N(\alpha)|\le1/(2\nrm{\alpha})\le1/(2\delta)$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:classical}; square.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Thus $\frac1N K_N$ is nonnegative, of total mass $1$, and
$O\bigl(1/(N\delta^2)\bigr)\to0$ outside any fixed neighbourhood of the
integers: precisely the approximate-identity property behind Fej\'er's theorem.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Transport to the continuous window]
For every $N,\alpha$,
\[
|C_N(\alpha)|^2=\Bigl(2\sum_{d=0}^{N-1}(N-d)\cos(2\pi d\alpha)-N\Bigr)\,|C_1(\alpha)|^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Square Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge} and substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:fejer}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithmic consequences}

\paragraph{Window-length design.} Given a required resolution $\Delta_{\min}$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:rayleigh} gives the exact minimal total observation
$2T>2c/\Delta_{\min}$ with $c\approx4.27836$, i.e.\ $>8.5567/\Delta_{\min}$.
The conventional rule $\Delta T\ge2\pi$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:null}) is safe but
wastes a factor $6.283/4.278\approx1.47$ in observation time.

\paragraph{Leakage budgeting.} By Theorems~\ref{thm:peak}--\ref{thm:sharp},
$|W(T,\omega)|/(2T)\le 1/(T|\omega|)$ and this is attained; thus a suppression
ratio $\rho$ requires $T|\omega|\ge1/\rho$, and no window-length increase alone
suppresses an interferer below $2/|\omega|$ in absolute terms.

\paragraph{Peak localization.} Theorem~\ref{thm:resol} shows half-peak occurs
only for $|\omega|\le2/T$; hence a grid of spacing $2/T$ guarantees a grid point
with response $\ge T$ near a true tone, and Theorem~\ref{thm:mainlow} localizes
it by inverting the monotone map $x\mapsto\sinc(x)$ on $[0,\pi]$.

\paragraph{Detecting arithmetic resonance.} Theorem~\ref{thm:arith} gives a
concrete $O(\log N)$ search: run the continued-fraction expansion of $\alpha$
for a denominator $q\le N$ with $\nrm{q\alpha}\le1/(N+1)$; then
$|S_M(q\alpha)|\ge(2/\pi)M$ for all $M\le(N+1)/2$.

\section{Discussion}

A qualitative resonance theorem --- the response is maximal at zero detuning ---
is a one-line corollary of the sinc law, but is strictly weaker than every
application above: it cannot locate the first zero, certify that the main lobe
is zero-free, bound leakage, certify that the leakage bound is tight, or decide
two-tone resolvability. In oscillatory-integral analysis the exact evaluation,
where available, dominates the extremal characterization.

The sampling bridge is not an approximation theorem but an identity, and it
explains why the sinc kernel of signal processing and the Dirichlet kernel of
Fourier analysis are the same object seen through different sampling. The
correspondence is summarized in Table~\ref{tab:corr}.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Continuous & Discrete\\
\midrule
$W(T,0)=2T$ & $S_N(k)=N$, $k\in\Z$\\
$W(T,\omega)=2\sin(\omega T)/\omega$ & $|S_N(\alpha)|=|\sin(\pi N\alpha)|/|\sin(\pi\alpha)|$\\
$|W|\le 2/|\omega|$ & $|S_N|\le 1/(2\nrm{\alpha})$\\
zeros at $\omega=k\pi/T$ & zeros at $\alpha=k/N$, $N\nmid k$\\
half-peak $\Rightarrow|\omega|\le2/T$ & half-peak $\Rightarrow\nrm{\alpha}\le1/N$\\
main lobe $\ge 2T\bigl(1-(\omega T)^2/4\bigr)$ & main lobe $\ge(2/\pi)N$, sharp\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The continuous--discrete correspondence.}\label{tab:corr}
\end{table}

The critical time--bandwidth product $c\approx4.27836$ appears to be less widely
known than it deserves, largely because the optics literature adopts the
convenient first-zero convention $\Delta T=2\pi$, which is sufficient but far
from necessary. The exactness here comes from the simplicity of
Lemma~\ref{lem:Gprime}.

\paragraph{Limitations.} All results concern the \emph{rectangular} window.
Tapered windows trade a wider main lobe for far faster sidelobe decay (Hann:
$|\omega|^{-3}$), and their exact analysis involves different kernels. The
analysis is also noise-free: it describes the deterministic matched-filter
response, not detection performance in additive noise.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Exact closed forms and sidelobe-sharpness statements for the Hann,
Hamming and Kaiser windows, with the analogue of $c$ for each; the Hann case
reduces to three shifted sinc kernels.
\item Multi-tone resolution: extend the criterion from two to $k$ equal tones on
an arithmetic grid.
\item Unequal amplitudes: determine $c(\lambda)$ for tones of amplitudes $1$ and
$\lambda<1$, and its asymptotics as $\lambda\to0$.
\item Higher-dimensional windows: product sinc kernels on rectangles, Bessel
(Airy) kernels on discs, with the threshold given by zeros of $J_1$.
\item Quantitative Weyl differencing: iterate the arithmetic resonance theorem
through van der Corput differencing with explicit constants.
\item Higher-order Fej\'er kernels (Jackson, de la Vall\'ee Poussin): generalize
the mass--concentration pair with $\delta^{-2}$ replaced by $\delta^{-2m}$.
\end{enumerate}

